    "response":  " To find the rate at which the water volume is changing, we need to use related rates. We start by expressing the volume of water in the conical tank as a function of its height.\n\nThe formula for the volume \\( V \\) of a cone is given by:\n\\[\nV = \\frac{1}{3} \\pi r^2 h\n\\]\n\nIn this problem, the radius \\( r \\) and height \\( h \\) of the water in the tank are not constant. However, we can relate \\( r \\) and \\( h \\) using the similar triangles formed by the entire cone and the smaller cone of water.\n\nThe ratio of the radius to the height of the entire cone is:\n\\[\n\\frac{r}{h} = \\frac{3}{6} = \\frac{1}{2}\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the radius \\( r \\) of the water at any height \\( h \\) is:\n\\[\nr = \\frac{1}{2} h\n\\]\n\nSubstitute \\( r = \\frac{1}{2} h \\) into the volume formula:\n\\[\nV = \\frac{1}{3} \\pi \\left( \\frac{1}{2} h \\right)^2 h\n\\]\n\\[\nV = \\frac{1}{3} \\pi \\left( \\frac{1}{4} h^2 \\right) h\n\\]\n\\[\nV = \\frac{1}{3} \\pi \\cdot \\frac{1}{4} h^3\n\\]\n\\[\nV = \\frac{1}{12} \\pi h^3\n\\]\n\nNext, we need to find the rate of change of volume with respect to time, \\( \\frac{dV}{dt} \\). We do this by differentiating \\( V \\) with respect to \\( t \\):\n\\[\n\\frac{dV}{dt} = \\frac{d}{dt} \\left( \\frac{1}{12} \\pi h^3 \\right)\n\\]\n\\[\n\\frac{dV}{dt} = \\frac{1}{12} \\pi \\cdot 3h^2 \\cdot \\frac{dh}{dt}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\frac{dV}{dt} = \\frac{1}{4} \\pi h^2 \\frac{dh}{dt}\n\\]\n\nWe are given that the water depth is decreasing at a rate of \\( \\frac{dh}{dt} = -0.04 \\) m/min when \\( h = 2 \\) m. Substitute these values into the equation:\n\\[\n\\frac{dV}{dt} = \\frac{1}{4} \\pi (2)^2 (-0.04)\n\\]\n\\[\n\\frac{dV}{dt} = \\frac{1}{4} \\pi \\cdot 4 \\cdot (-0.04)\n\\]\n\\[\n\\frac{dV}{dt} = \\pi \\cdot (-0.04)\n\\]\n\\[\n\\frac{dV}{dt} = -0.04\\pi\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the rate at which the water volume is changing at that instant is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{-0.04\\pi}\n\\] m^3/min. This negative sign indicates that the volume of water is decreasing.",
